[
  "[Tell me about yourself]\n\nI’m a Senior Product Designer based in Barcelona.\n\nI co-founded Kings of Mambo, a UX studio that I ran for eleven years. I worked across research, interaction and visual design, prototyping, and delivery for clients in several industries.\n\nAt Abacum, I worked on data integrations and later financial modeling. One of my main projects was Dataset Manager, where I led design and customer testing to help finance teams configure their data more independently.\n\nMost recently, I was the sole designer at Remuner, a sales-compensation platform. My scope covered data configuration, compensation plans, rep and manager experiences, approvals, and permissions. I also designed its conversational AI agent and built the design system.\n\nThe common thread is making complex products understandable and usable. For my next role, I’m looking to help define the problem, explore options with Product and Engineering, and stay involved through delivery.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Why Alan]\n\nWhat attracts me to Alan is the mission of making healthcare easier to access and navigate. From the role description, Alan Clinic sounds like an interesting design challenge: different people, processes, and rules need to work together, while the experience should remain clear for the member.\n\nThat connects with what I enjoy most in design. At Abacum and Remuner, I worked on making complex logic understandable and usable. Healthcare would be a new domain for me, but the challenge of coordinating different actors and making complicated processes feel simple is familiar.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Working with Product and Engineering]\n\nI work closely with Product and Engineering from the beginning of a project. Before I start designing, I involve them to understand the problem, the project requirements, and any technical constraints.\n\nI keep them involved as the design develops, rather than waiting until handoff. I share work in progress so we can check that the direction still makes sense and identify anything that might be difficult to build.\n\nIf a constraint comes up, we can discuss the options while the design is still evolving. That helps me avoid making assumptions and discovering important issues too late.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Walk me through a project — Dataset Manager]\n\nA good example is Dataset Manager at Abacum, a financial-planning platform.\n\nFinance teams needed to configure and transform their data before using it in their models. The existing setup relied on technical configuration files, so customers depended on the implementation team. Our goal was to make standard configuration something they could do more independently.\n\nI led the design and customer testing, working with Product and Engineering.\n\nAn early version combined a simpler interface with SQL for writing transformations. Testing showed that users understood what they wanted to do with their data. They could explain the logic using Excel functions, but struggled to express it in SQL. I interpreted that as a mismatch between the product's language and their existing knowledge.\n\nI brought that finding to Product and Engineering. Together, we moved toward guided configuration and extended Abacum's existing modeling language, keeping it close to the spreadsheet concepts users already knew.\n\nWe also addressed friction within the workflow. For example, users had to leave their dataset to create a missing dimension, then return and continue. I proposed letting them create and edit basic dimensions within the dataset, while keeping advanced management separate.\n\nThe changes shipped. We had qualitative reports of customers reaching a working configuration in their first session, and customers reported less confusion and fewer errors after the dimension change. Those were qualitative outcomes, rather than instrumented measurements.\n\nThe central design decision was to build around how finance users already understood their work, rather than asking them to learn a technical language just to complete it.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Impact / research changed direction]\n\nAt Abacum, I led design and customer testing for a tool that helped finance teams configure their own data.\n\nBefore that, they depended on implementation specialists and technical configuration files. The goal was to give them more independence.\n\nOur early direction combined a simpler interface with SQL. But testing showed something important: users could explain the transformation they needed using Excel functions, yet they struggled to express it in SQL.\n\nI took that finding to Product and Engineering. Together, we changed direction and extended the existing modeling language around concepts familiar to finance users.\n\nThe guided workflow shipped. Our recollection was that customers could reach a working configuration in their first session. The implementation team also felt standard setups took roughly half the previous time. That was team feedback, not an analytics measurement.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Customer feedback / small change with impact]\n\nAt Abacum, I identified a problem that forced users to leave the task they were working on.\n\nWhen configuring a dataset, they could assign dimensions to its columns. But if a dimension was missing, they had to leave the dataset, create it somewhere else, and then come back. Customers reported that this caused confusion and errors.\n\nI proposed putting basic dimension creation and editing directly into the open dataset. Product and Engineering agreed to build it.\n\nWe kept advanced management in the separate area, so the first version addressed the immediate workflow problem without moving everything.\n\nThe change shipped, and customers reported faster configuration, fewer errors, and less confusion. We didn't have a measured baseline, so I would describe that as qualitative customer feedback.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Pushback / influence / capacity tradeoff]\n\nAt Abacum, Engineering was concerned that a design system would compete with squad priorities and never make it into production.\n\nI co-led the business case with the Head of Design. We showed examples of inconsistencies across the product and compared different ways to staff the work.\n\nThe team chose a focused Maker Days week. That meant more people stepping away from squad work, but for a shorter period.\n\nMy contribution included the business case and parts of the token and component-library work. Engineering built the original implementation pipeline.\n\nDuring that week, the token setup and the first Button reached production. That gave us concrete evidence that the path from design to code worked. It didn't prove financial return or complete the wider migration.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Disagreement / mixed feedback]\n\nAt Remuner, research on the manager view showed some basic workflow problems. Managers lacked an overview by metric, had to navigate through too many steps, and couldn't easily compare previous periods.\n\nI recommended fixing those problems before doing a broader redesign. The CEO and CTO chose to proceed with the full redesign instead.\n\nI explained my disagreement, then accepted the decision and worked to keep the research findings in the design. That included improving the metric overview and navigation.\n\nThe redesign shipped. Feedback was positive about those improvements, but the overall response was mixed. Some users still struggled to find information they needed.\n\nI captured and analysed that feedback, separating problems that blocked tasks from other friction to guide future iterations. I can't claim that later iterations resolved them.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Mistake / accountability / learning]\n\nAt Remuner, I deleted a token file because I thought it was duplicated. The frontend still depended on it, and the next deployment caused a short production outage.\n\nThe mistake was my assumption that the file was safe to remove.\n\nI restored it and pushed the fix. For the next several days, I manually checked that the required files were present before deployment.\n\nThe team then added a required-file check while the dependency still needed to be cleaned up.\n\nThe service was restored, and the checking process changed. I don't have an exact outage duration or a measured customer impact.\n\nIf I were making that change again, I would verify which parts of the application depend on the file before treating it as a duplicate.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[AI / scope / trust / zero to one]\n\nAt Remuner, I designed a conversational agent for questions about commissions and sales plans.\n\nAs the sole designer, I owned the interface, conversation design, answer formats, and design-side boundaries. Engineering owned the technical architecture, and we agreed the scope with the founders. There wasn't a PM on this project.\n\nWe started from the data Engineering could make available. I designed the experience around supported questions, refusals for questions outside that scope, and different contexts for reps and managers.\n\nReps could ask about their own plans. Managers could also ask about their teams. Answers used tables or lists when that made the information easier to understand.\n\nThe agent launched to all customers. Feedback was mixed, and we continued improving tone, terminology, and presentation. I don't have reliable adoption or accuracy metrics, so I wouldn't present the launch as proof that every answer was correct.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Vague feedback / clarify the real problem]\n\nAt Remuner, we received feedback that the dashboard for sales reps wasn't intuitive enough. I didn't want to assume that meant we needed to redesign the interface.\n\nI identified the users who had given that feedback and arranged interviews to understand where they were getting stuck.\n\nThe same complaint described different problems. One user didn't understand how their compensation plan was calculated. Another couldn't tell which metrics affected it. A third saw a commission amount but didn't know when they would receive it, because their payout covered a three-month period.\n\nWe compared those findings with our existing evidence and prioritised the problems by severity, focusing on changes that could help users quickly.\n\nAs the sole designer, I designed all of the changes. For example, I added access to a summary of the plan calculation, with a route to the full detail. For quarterly payouts, I designed a period selector and information about payment timing and when the period would close.\n\nThe changes shipped. In testing, users could understand the information they had previously been missing. That gave us qualitative evidence that the changes addressed those specific problems.\n\nThe important point was turning one vague complaint into separate, actionable problems, each with its own solution.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n",
  "[Why did you leave Remuner?]\n\nMy role at Remuner ended following a company decision. From my perspective, there was a mismatch in how product decisions were made and how much design could contribute to defining the problem. I’m looking for a role where that discussion happens earlier, with Product and Engineering.\n\n[pause — let them ask]\n"
]
